                            <comment id="498376" author="green" created="Thu, 23 Jul 2026 06:39:14 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;I think this python regexp stuff is terribly fragile and might create more problems than it solves.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="498479" author="stancheff" created="Fri, 24 Jul 2026 10:36:04 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;I am a bit biased.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Linux kernel headers are very stable, however ports done by distros can be a bit more random (functions converting to #define macros when porting patches, rhel9 for example has a few cases of this). Once done those ports are also quite stable. There is general pushback against trivial changes which helps keep things stable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both the build snippet checks with configure and the regex checking python snippets have weaknesses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The configure compile checks are slower and bit harder to debug, although the parallel version is a bit easier to iterate over quickly now with &lt;tt&gt;PARALLEL_BUILD_OPT=debug&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of the regex style checks got a little sloppy (I just did builds against everything to be satisfied that they were working but that probably is not enough to catch some dumb errors)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I think maybe adding more detail into the final config header and/or output during configure will help catch unexpected behaviour such as failed matches and/or incorrect matches.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
